1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,490
This is FoodBridge, our expiry-aware food donation allocator, built in Java.

2
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:13,578
In the Java program, we load the limited storage scenario and run the allocation.

3
00:00:13,428 --> 00:00:19,910
Every record is saved in an SQLite database, and loaded again when the program restarts.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,554
The web GUI follows the same rules.

5
00:00:23,404 --> 00:00:28,019
The dashboard shows every donor and food item, ordered by expiry.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,930
Our Min-Heap keeps the food that expires first at the top.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,352
Now we run the smart allocation.

8
00:00:36,202 --> 00:00:42,960
Each delivery gives the smallest of food, storage space and need, and the map draws every route.

9
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:50,990
The report shows the impact, and the database screen lets us query every table with SQL.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,064
FoodBridge.

11
00:00:54,914 --> 00:00:57,559
Saving food, serving humanity.

